</NOSCRIPT></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>The format of=20
      this article does not allow me to include complete working =
examples of=20
      each of the ten new features I describe. I do include extensive =
code=20
      fragments to illustrate how to use each of the new features, but =
don't=20
      expect to be able to compile and run the code as shown.</P>
      <H3>10. Parsing XML</H3>
      <P>The ability to parse XML in Java is hugely important, and the =
only=20
      reason that this new feature isn't ranked higher on my list is =
that the=20
      JAXP API for parsing XML has been available as a standard =
extension for a=20
      while. What is new in Java 1.4, however, is that JAXP has been =
added to=20
      the core platform, which is important because it makes XML parsing =

      ubiquitous. The JAXP parsing API is defined in=20
      <CODE>javax.xml.parsers</CODE> and includes classes for SAX and =
DOM=20
      parsing, which are used with the <CODE>org.xml.sax</CODE> packages =
(and=20
      its sub-packages) and with the <CODE>org.w3c.dom</CODE> package; =
these=20
      have also been added to Java 1.4. Here's how you could use the =
JAXP API to=20
      parse an XML document into a DOM tree, and then use the DOM API to =
extract=20
      information from that document.</P><PRE><CODE>import java.io.*;
import javax.xml.parsers.*;
import org.w3c.dom.*;

File f;  // The file to parse.  Assume this is initialized elsewhere

// Create a factory object for creating DOM parsers
DocumentBuilderFactory factory =3D DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
// Now use the factory to create a DOM parser (a.k.a. a DocumentBuilder)
DocumentBuilder parser =3D factory.newDocumentBuilder();
// Parse the file and build a Document tree to represent its content
Document document =3D parser.parse(f);
// Ask the document for a list of all &lt;sect1&gt; tags it contains
NodeList sections =3D document.getElementsByTagName("sect1");
// Loop through those &lt;sect1&gt; elements one at a time, and extract =
the
// content of their &lt;h3&gt; tags.
int numSections =3D sections.getLength();
for(int i =3D 0; i &lt; numSections; i++) {
    Element section =3D (Element)sections.item(i);  // A &lt;sect1&gt;
    // Find the first element child of this section (a &lt;h3&gt; =
element)
    // and print the text it contains.
    Node title =3D section.getFirstChild();
    while(title !=3D null &amp;&amp; title.getNodeType() !=3D =
Node.ELEMENT_NODE)=20
	title =3D title.getNextSibling();
    // Print the text contained in the Text node child of this element
    if (title!=3Dnull) =
System.out.println(title.getFirstChild().getNodeValue());
}</CODE></PRE>
      <H3>9. Transforming XML</H3>
      <P>The JAXP API includes classes for XML transformations as well =
as XML=20
      parsing. <CODE>javax.xml.transform</CODE> and its sub-packages =
allow you=20
      to change an XML document from one representation (as a stream of =
XML=20
      tags, as a stream of SAX events, or as a DOM tree) to another=20
      representation, and it allows you to apply an XSLT transformation =
to the=20
      document content at the same time. It is very easy to use, and =
like the=20
      XML parsing feature listed above, this would be higher up on the =
list if=20
      it hadn't been available as a standard extension before Java 1.4 =
was=20
      released.</P>
      <P>The code below shows how you could apply an XSLT transformation =
to a=20
      DOM document tree and output the transformed document as XML text =
to=20
      standard output.</P><PRE><CODE>import javax.xml.transform.*;
import javax.xml.transform.dom.*;
import javax.xml.transform.stream.*;
import org.w3c.dom.*;

File xsltfile;      // An XSLT file; initialized elsewhere
Document document;  // Assume we've already read or created this DOM =
document

Source xsltSource =3D new StreamSource(xsltfile); // Source for =
transformation
Source source =3D new DOMSource(document);        // Document to be =
transformed
Result result =3D new StreamResult(System.out);   // Where to put result =
document

// Start off with a factory object
TransformerFactory tf =3D TransformerFactory.newInstance();
// Use the factory to read the XSLT file into a Templates object
Templates transformation =3D tf.newTemplates(xsltSource);
// Create a Transformer object from the Templates object
Transformer transformer =3D transformation.newTransformer();
// Finally, perform the transformation
transformer.transform(source, result);</PRE></CODE>
      <H3>8. Preferences</H3>
      <P>Many of the important new features of Java 1.4 are new =
utilities in or=20
      beneath the <CODE>java.util</CODE> package. =
<CODE>java.util.prefs</CODE>=20
      is one such utility: a facility for persistently storing and =
querying=20
      user-specific preferences and system-wide application =
configuration=20
      information. The key class is <CODE>Preferences</CODE>, which =
represents a=20
      persistent set of named preferences for a package (all classes in =
a=20
      package typically share the same user-specific and system-wide=20
      <CODE>Preferences</CODE> object). <CODE>Preferences</CODE> defines =
static=20
      methods for obtaining the user and system <CODE>Preferences</CODE> =
objects=20
      for a package, and defines instance methods for setting and =
querying named=20
      values of various types. The following is typical code that an =
application=20
      might use to query preferences when initializing itself. The cool =
thing=20
      about the Preferences API is that it is just so easy to use -- all =
of the=20
      hard work of managing configuration files just goes =
away.</P><PRE><CODE>// Get Preferences objects for user and system =
preferences for the package
// that contains the TextEditor class.
Preferences userprefs =3D =
Preferences.userNodeForPackage(TextEditor.class);
Preferences sysprefs =3D =
Preferences.systemNodeForPackage(TextEditor.class);

// Look up a user preference value as an integer.
// Note that we always pass a default value
int width =3D userprefs.getInt("width", 80);
// Look up a user preference using a system preference as the default
String dictionary =3D userprefs.get("dictionary"
                                  sysprefs.get("dictionary",
                                               =
"default_dictionary"));</CODE></PRE>
