      <P>Logging is another important utility, defined by the=20
      <CODE>java.util.logging</CODE> package. It is particularly useful =
for=20
      servers and other applications that run unattended or do not have =
a user=20
      interface. In typical usage, the application developer uses a=20
      <CODE>Logger</CODE> object with a name that corresponds to the =
class or=20
      package name of the application to generate log messages -- at any =
of=20
      seven severity levels (defined by the <CODE>Level</CODE> =
class).</P>
      <P>These messages might report errors and warnings, or provide=20
      informational messages about interesting events in the =
application's=20
      lifecycle. They can include debugging information, or even trace =
the=20
      execution of important methods within the program. It is not =
intended that=20
      all of these messages will actually be generated and logged all of =
the=20
      time; there is a default logging configuration file that specifies =
where=20
      log messages are directed to (the console, a file, a network =
socket, or a=20
      combination of these), how they are formatted (as plain text or =
XML=20
      documents), and at what severity threshold they are logged (log =
messages=20
      with a severity below the specified threshold are discarded with =
very=20
      little overhead and should not significantly impact the =
performance of the=20
      application.) The system administrator or end user of the =
application can=20
      modify this default configuration to suit their needs.</P>
      <P>The <CODE>java.util.logging</CODE> package is very flexible, =
and can be=20
      used in a number of ways. For most applications, however, use of =
the=20
      Logging API is quite simple. Obtain a named <CODE>Logger</CODE> =
object=20
      whenever necessary by calling the static =
<CODE>Logger.getLogger()</CODE>=20
      method, passing the class or package name of the application as =
the logger=20
      name. Then use one of the many <CODE>Logger</CODE> instance =
methods to=20
      generate log messages. The easiest methods to use have names that=20
      correspond to severity levels, such as <CODE>severe()</CODE>,=20
      <CODE>warning()</CODE>, <CODE>info()</CODE>, and =
<CODE>debug()</CODE>:</P><PRE><CODE>import java.util.logging.*;

// Get a Logger object named after the current package.
Logger logger =3D Logger.getLogger("com.davidflanagan.servers.pop");
logger.info("Starting server.");       // Log an informational message
ServerSocket ss;
try { ss =3D new ServerSocket(110); }
catch(Exception e) {                   // Log exceptions
  logger.log(Level.SEVERE, "Can't bind port 110", e);  // complex log =
message
  logger.warning("Exiting");                           // simple warning =

  return;
}
logger.fine("got server socket");  // low severity (fine detail) debug =
message</CODE></PRE>
      <H3>6. Secure Sockets and HTTPS</H3>
      <P>One of Java's greatest strengths, ever since Java 1.0, has been =
the=20
      ability to perform powerful networking with very little code. Java =
1.4=20
      adds support for secure sockets (using the SSL and TLS protocols) =
so that=20
      you can now easily write powerful <I>and secure</I> networking =
code. SSL=20
      support is provided by the <CODE>javax.net.ssl</CODE> package. =
(Note:=20
      <CODE>javax</CODE> not <CODE>java</CODE>.) As with all =
security-related=20
      packages in Java, this package is complex and highly configurable. =

      Fortunately, however, the most common uses of SSL are quite easy =
to=20
      implement. The following code sets up an SSL socket to securely=20
      communicate with a Web server, using the HTTPS =
protocol:</P><PRE><CODE>import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
 =20
// Get a SocketFactory object for creating SSL sockets
SSLSocketFactory factory =3D (SSLSocketFactory) =
SSLSocketFactory.getDefault();

// Create a secure socket connected to the HTTPS port (port 443) of a =
server
SSLSocket sslsock =3D (SSLSocket) factory.createSocket(hostname, 443);

// Get the certificate presented by the web server.  This may throw an=20
// exception if the server didn't supply a certificate.  Look at the
// issuer of the certificate and decide if it is trusted.
SSLSession session =3D sslsock.getSession();
X509Certificate cert =3D =
(X509Certificate)session.getPeerCertificates()[0];
String issuer =3D cert.getIssuerDN().getName();=20

// Assuming we trust the certificate, we now use the socket just like a =
normal
// java.net.Socket object.  So send a HTTP request and read the response
PrintWriter out =3D new PrintWriter(sslsock.getOutputStream());
out.print("GET " + args[1] + " HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n");
out.flush();

// Next, read the server's response and print it to the console.
BufferedReader in =3D
  new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(sslsock.getInputStream()));
String line;
while((line =3D in.readLine()) !=3D null) System.out.println(line);

// Finally, close the socket.
sslsock.close(); </CODE></PRE>
      <H3>5. LinkedHashMap</H3>
      <P>Halfway through my list of cool features of Java 1.4 is the=20
      <CODE>java.util.LinkedHashMap</CODE> class. This new addition to =
the=20
      Collections API is a <CODE>Map</CODE> implementation that is based =
on a=20
      hashtable, just as <CODE>HashMap</CODE> is. =
<CODE>LinkedHashMap</CODE>=20
      differs in that it also maintains a linked list running through =
the map=20
      entries, and so can iterate through those entries in a predictable =
order.=20
      Usually, this order is the order in which entries are inserted =
into the=20
      map; you may occasionally encounter situations in which this is a =
very=20
      useful feature. Java 1.4 also includes a =
<CODE>LinkedHashSet</CODE> which=20
      is very similar.</P>
      <P>If the idea of merging a <CODE>HashMap</CODE> with a=20
      <CODE>LinkedList</CODE> is not cool enough for you, consider this: =

      <CODE>LinkedHashMap</CODE> can be configured (pass =
<CODE>true</CODE> as=20
      the third argument to the constructor) to order its entries from=20
      most-recently-accessed (i.e. queried or set) to =
least-recently-accessed.=20
      That's a nifty feature, but there's more! =
<CODE>LinkedHashMap</CODE> has a=20
      protected method named <CODE>removeEldestEntry()</CODE> that is =
called=20
      every time a new entry is added to the map. If this method returns =

      <CODE>true</CODE>, then the "eldest" entry in the map (i.e. the =
one that=20
      was least-recently inserted or least-recently used) is =
automatically=20
      deleted. The default implementation of =
<CODE>removeEldestEntry()</CODE>=20
      always returns <CODE>false</CODE>, but you can override it to =
return=20
      <CODE>true</CODE> when the size of the map reaches a certain =
maximum size.=20
      Presto: you've got an LRU (least-recently-used) =
cache!</P><PRE><CODE>public class LRUCache extends =
java.util.LinkedHashMap {
    public LRUCache(int maxsize) {
	super(maxsize*4/3 + 1, 0.75f, true);
	this.maxsize =3D maxsize;
    }
    protected int maxsize;
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry() { return size() &gt; maxsize; =
}
}</CODE></PRE>
      <P><I>Editor's Update: The signature for =
<CODE>removeEldestEntry()</CODE>=20
      does not match that of the superclass and therefore this method =
does not=20
      override the corresponding method in the superclass. A fix that =
also=20
      includes the J2SE 1.5 feature metadata has been posted by David =
Flanagan=20
      as a blog entry <A=20
      href=3D"http://www.davidflanagan.com/blog/000014.html">Failure to =
override=20
      </A>.</I></P>
